## FAQ

**Q: What is the difference between a schematic review and a PCB design review?**
A: A schematic review evaluates the electrical design intent: component selection, circuit topology, protection, and signal connectivity. A PCB design review evaluates the physical implementation: trace routing, clearances, stack-up, DFM, and compliance.

**Q: At what stage should we engage Qmax for a design review?**
A: Highest ROI at two stages: after schematic completion (before layout begins) and after PCB layout completion (before Gerber release).

**Q: What file formats does Qmax accept for reviews?**
A: Native EDA files from Altium, Cadence Allegro/OrCAD, Mentor PADS/Xpedition, KiCad, and Eagle. Also PDF schematics, Gerber RS-274X, ODB++, IPC-2581.

**Q: What does DFM review specifically check on a PCB layout?**
A: Minimal trace width/spacing, via annular rings, copper-to-board-edge clearance, soldermask expansion, impedance-controlled layer assignment, drill aspect ratios, and feature tolerances.

**Q: How long does a typical PCB design review take?**
A: Standard schematic reviews (100–300 components): 3–5 business days. PCB layout reviews: 5–7 business days.

**Q: Can Qmax reconstruct a schematic from a physical PCBA?**
A: Yes. Reverse engineering using component ID, X-ray imaging for multi-layer net tracing, and connectivity mapping to produce a functional schematic and EDA-native design database in 10–15 business days.

**Q: What EDA toolchains does Qmax support for CAD migration?**
A: Altium, Cadence Allegro/OrCAD, Mentor PADS/Xpedition, and KiCad. Legacy tools include PROTEL, P-CAD, and legacy OrCAD.

**Q: How does Qmax approach BoM obsolescence management?**
A: Lifecycle status queried against industry databases (SiliconExpert, IHS Markit). NRND/Obsolete parts escalated for replacement qualification.

**Q: What compliance standards does Qmax address?**
A: CE Marking (EMC, LVD, RED), FCC Parts 15/68, IEC 61000 EMC series, IEC 62368-1 safety, UL 508A, ISO 26262 functional safety, RoHS 3, REACH.

**Q: Can Qmax identify the root cause of a radiated emissions failure?**
A: Yes. Exceedance sources identified (inadequate filtering, common-mode issues, switching harmonics), correlated with design, targeted modifications defined.

**Q: What is DVT and how is it different from design verification?**
A: Verification confirms implementation matches spec (simulation/unit test). DVT (Design Validation Testing) confirms product meets requirements under real-world stress conditions.

**Q: How does Qmax structure pricing for design review services?**
A: Fixed-fee basis per engagement. Pricing based on component count, layer count, and signal speed. Initial 1-hour consultations complimentary.